    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate focusing on high-ceiling production against vulnerable arms. The premier play is **Corbin Carroll** (74.79), whose matchup against Landen Roupp is our primary engine for points and stolen bases. Despite the presence of a respectable arm behind the dish, **Corbin Carroll**\u2019s elite sprint speed and favorable batting order placement make him an essential anchor. Alongside him, **Josh Naylor** (64.72) and **Austin Riley** (63.51) offer strong production profiles; **Austin Riley** specifically benefits from an immense matchup advantage against Garrett\u2019s 9.71 xERA, while **Josh Naylor** thrives against the pedestrian arm of Anthony Kay. We are also monitoring **Mickey Moniak** (60.43), whose park factors boost him into the \"Strong\" category despite a minor platoon disadvantage.\n\nOur bench strategy remains disciplined, prioritizing efficiency over blind volume. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (39.37) was the closest to cracking the lineup but ultimately fell below our 40-point floor due to a disadvantageous platoon matchup against Suarez and unfavorable wind conditions. Meanwhile, we continue to carry **Jose Caballero**, **Trevor Story**, and **Giancarlo Stanton** on the IL, while keeping organizational depth players like **Max Muncy**, **Carson Williams**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Kyle Karros**, **Alex Freeland**, and **Henry Bolte** in reserve as we navigate the daily cap. We remain cautious with **Tyler Soderstrom** (41.85) and **Dalton Rushing** (54.96), as both are currently waiting on official lineup confirmation; should **Tyler Soderstrom** shift lower than the projected #5 spot, his score may dip further, necessitating a late-afternoon reevaluation of our bench utility options. **Brice Turang** (56.74) remains a vital start despite a tough matchup, as his 100% stolen base expectation against Brown provides unique value that raw averages cannot capture.",
